  "Welcome to Chapter 6 — Types of Democracy. In this topic we look at direct and representative democracy, parliamentary and presidential democracy, four student councils that behave like four different countries, and how five real democracies actually function. Press Begin Learning when you are ready.": "audio/en/b65a403a4aed8e74.mp3",
  "Team B has each class elect a representative, and those representatives then choose the President and decide together. Nobody governs directly; they act through people they elected. That is representative democracy — and because the President comes out of the elected body, it is closest to the parliamentary kind.": "audio/en/9808c6db411ed24b.mp3",
  "The United Kingdom is a representative democracy — a parliamentary democracy with a constitutional monarchy — in which executive power lies with the Prime Minister. The Monarch is the Head of State and the Prime Minister the Head of Government, and its key features are an unwritten constitution, parliamentary sovereignty, and a multi-party system.": "audio/en/02234edbcdad8c93.mp3",
  "In a parliamentary democracy, members of the Executive are also part of the legislature, and the Executive is accountable to the legislature — people elect the legislature but not the executive. India and Canada are the examples given. In a presidential democracy, by contrast, the Executive is independent of the legislature and the President is elected by, and accountable to, the people.": "audio/en/3a6969eec37977ca.mp3",
  "The four student councils show that systems may differ in how power is shared, who participates in decision-making, and whether checks and balances are in place. Team A practises direct democracy, Team B is closest to a parliamentary system, Team C resembles a presidential system, and Team D — where only selected candidates may contest and opposition voices are discouraged — shows that holding elections is not, by itself, enough to make a system truly democratic.": "audio/en/d064c4f910d5f826.mp3",
  "In a direct democracy, citizens directly participate in most decision-making processes, though this is difficult to follow in large countries — Switzerland is the example. In a representative, or indirect, democracy, people elect their representatives at periodic elections, those representatives govern on their behalf, and the government remains accountable to the people — India is the example.": "audio/en/2aae08316eceac0b.mp3",
  "But direct democracy is difficult to follow in large countries.": "audio/en/13a02f5ddb968573.mp3",
  "Switzerland is the example given of a direct democracy, where citizens directly participate in most decision-making processes — though such a system is difficult to follow in large countries. India is the example of a representative or indirect democracy, where people elect their representatives, do not directly govern, and the government is accountable to the people.": "audio/en/ed85833162e71702.mp3",
  "Both India and the United States elect their governments. So why does an Indian vote only for a legislature, while an American also votes separately for a President?": "audio/en/a0924253fe2fc539.mp3",
  "In a parliamentary democracy, members of the Executive are also part of the legislature and are accountable to it, so people elect the legislature but not the executive directly — India and Canada are the examples. In a presidential democracy, the Executive is independent of the legislature, and the President is elected by, and accountable to, the people — the United States of America is the example.": "audio/en/10dfb8c4e991165e.mp3",
  "It is simply the law-making body of a country: the assembly of members who debate a proposal and then vote it into law.": "audio/en/2aa340f723aa6572.mp3",
